  "Question": "Simplify \\(\\left(\\sqrt[6]{27}-\\sqrt{6\\frac34}\\right)^2.\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\frac34\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\frac{3\\sqrt3}{4}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac32\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\frac{3\\sqrt3}{2}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Simplify both roots first, because they become multiples of the same square root. All roots here denote their nonnegative values. The sixth root is the one-sixth power, so, using \\(27=3^3\\),\n\\[\n\\sqrt[6]{27}=(3^3)^{1/6}=3^{3/6}=\\sqrt3.\n\\]\nAlso, the mixed number \\(6\\frac34\\) equals \\(\\frac{27}{4}\\). Factoring \\(27=9\\cdot3\\) lets us take the square roots of \\(9\\) and \\(4\\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt{6\\frac34}=\\sqrt{\\frac{27}{4}}=\\frac{3\\sqrt3}{2}.\n\\]\nNow combine the like radical terms before squaring:\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left(\\sqrt3-\\frac{3\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)^2\n&=\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt3}{2}\\right)^2\\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{4}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThe answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac34}\\), choice \\(\\boxed{\\text{A}}\\).",
